class Solution {
  public int tallerKeycapsThresholdFile(int[] readings, int k) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[]distance=new int[a.length];Deque<Integer>stack=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++){while(!stack.isEmpty()&&a[stack.peek()]<a[i]){int j=stack.pop();distance[j]=i-j;}stack.push(i);}for(int x:distance)out.add(x);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)if(x>=k)answer++;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build the result list

For each number in readings, look to its right. Find the first number that is larger, not equal. Record how many places to the right it is. Record 0 if no larger number is found. Keep the answers in input order.

Return the answer

Count the numbers in the result list that are at least k (greater than or equal to k). Return that count. An empty list gives 0.

Why this works

Each value in the result list follows Step 1. Step 2 uses only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the result list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

